Types of US Working Visas — Recruiter Cheat Sheet
🔹 1. H-1B Visa – Specialty Occupation
• Most common in IT hiring.
• For highly skilled professionals (IT, Engineering, Finance, etc.).
• Must have a Bachelor’s degree or equivalent in a related field.
• Employer sponsors the visa.
• Valid for 3 years, extendable to 6 years.
• H1B workers can work on C2C or W2 depending on setup  
• Valid for 3 years, renewable.
• No sponsorship hassle — quick processing.
🧠 You might see profiles saying “TN eligible” — that means easy onboarding.
🔹 4. EAD (Employment Authorization Document)
• Work permit that allows non-citizens to work legally in the US.
• Issued to many dependent visa holders or pending Green Card applicants.
• Types of EADs include:
o H4 EAD – for H1B dependents (spouse)
o L2 EAD – for L1 dependents
o OPT / STEM OPT EAD – for international students
o GC EAD – pending Green Card applicants
🧠 You’ll see many candidates on “OPT EAD” or “H4 EAD” for entry-level or contract roles.
🔹 5. OPT / CPT (Student Visas under F1)
• F1 Visa = Student visa
• CPT (Curricular Practical Training): Work authorization during study.
• OPT (Optional Practical Training): 12-month post-graduation work period.
• STEM OPT Extension: Extra 24 months for STEM graduates (total 3 years).
🧠 OPT/STEM OPT candidates often work on W2, ideal for contract positions.
🔹 6. Green Card (GC) – Permanent Resident
• Holder can work for any employer without sponsorship.
• Permanent work authorization.
• Many GC holders work on C2C (own companies) or W2.
🧠 Easy onboarding — no visa transfer needed.
🔹 7. US Citizen (USC)
• Natural-born or naturalized citizen.
• Can work anywhere, any role.
• No restrictions on C2C/W2/1099.
🧠 Always a preferred category for clients (no sponsorship or compliance risk).
🔹 8. Others (Less Common in IT but Good to Know)
Visa Description
O-1 Visa For individuals with extraordinary ability (e.g., Scientists, Artists, Engineers).
E-3 Visa For Australian nationals in specialty occupations (similar to H1B).
H-2B Visa Temporary non-agricultural workers (hospitality, construction, etc.).
J-1 Visa Exchange visitor visa (interns, trainees, researchers).
